  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🗺️ Fantasy Country Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Kingdoms, empires and island realms, from Valdheim to the Corvium Empire."
    },
    "intro": "A country's name tells you who lives there before anyone speaks: <em>Mirlond</em> sounds elvish, <em>Valdheim</em> Norse, <em>Corvium</em> like an empire with roads and legions. This **fantasy country name generator** builds names in six styles, each from the sounds of a real or imagined language, and can add a title to match: a kingdom, a grand duchy, a dread dominion.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How real countries got their names",
        "content": "<p>Most real country names come from one of four places, and fantasy names can use the same ones:</p><ul><li><strong>A people.</strong> England is the land of the Angles; France is named after the Franks. Denmark is \"the march of the Danes\", <em>mark</em> meaning a border land.</li><li><strong>The land itself.</strong> The Netherlands are the low lands, Montenegro the black mountain, Iceland just what it says.</li><li><strong>Something precious.</strong> Argentina comes from the Latin <em>argentum</em>, silver, which explorers hoped to find there.</li><li><strong>A person.</strong> Bolivia is named after Simón Bolívar, the Philippines after Philip II of Spain.</li></ul><p>Tolkien worked the same way in Elvish: <em>Gondor</em> means \"stone-land\" and <em>Mordor</em> \"black land\".</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Six styles",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Elvish and flowing</strong>: soft sounds and endings like <em>-dor</em> (land), <em>-lond</em> (haven) and <em>-wen</em>. <em>Celanor</em>, <em>Silwen</em>.</li><li><strong>Northern and Norse</strong>: endings from the Scandinavian languages. <em>-heim</em> is home or world (Norse myth has Jotunheim and Vanaheim), <em>-gard</em> an enclosure (Midgard, Asgard), <em>-rike</em> a realm, <em>-vik</em> a bay. <em>Skarvik</em>, <em>Ravnholm</em>.</li><li><strong>Imperial and Latin</strong>: <a href=\"roman-name-generator.html\">Roman</a> names ended in <em>-ia</em> (Gallia, Britannia, Hispania), and these follow suit. <em>Severica</em>, <em>Aurenna</em>, <em>Corvium</em>.</li><li><strong>Old English kingdoms</strong>: before England, there were Wessex, Mercia and Northumbria. These use <a href=\"anglo-saxon-name-generator.html\">Anglo-Saxon</a> name parts like <em>aelf</em> (elf), <em>wulf</em> and <em>beorn</em> (warrior). <em>Aelfwold</em>, <em>Wulfmarch</em>.</li><li><strong>Dark and dangerous</strong>: hard consonants and growling endings for the enemy across the mountains. <em>Drakgrim</em>, <em>Nekoth</em>, <em>Malkhal</em>.</li><li><strong>Islands and the sea</strong>: island words like <em>-holm</em> and <em>-ey</em>, and <em>-ennis</em>, which echoes the Welsh <em>ynys</em> and Cornish <em>enys</em>, both \"island\". <em>Gwynholm</em>, <em>Tolennis</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Kingdom, empire or republic?",
        "content": "<p>Choose \"With a title\" and the name comes with a form of government, which says a lot about the place:</p><ul><li><strong>Kingdom</strong> and <strong>High Kingdom</strong>: one crown, or a king over other kings.</li><li><strong>Empire</strong> and <strong>Imperium</strong>: rule over other peoples, usually won by conquest.</li><li><strong>Grand Duchy</strong> and <strong>Principality</strong>: smaller states, often squeezed between bigger ones.</li><li><strong>Marches</strong>: border country, held by lords whose job is to keep the neighbours out.</li><li><strong>Jarldoms</strong> and <strong>Thanes</strong>: a land of chieftains rather than a single throne.</li><li><strong>Most Serene Republic</strong>: the title of Venice, a merchant state run by its rich families.</li><li><strong>Thalassocracy</strong>: from the Greek for \"sea power\", a realm whose strength is its fleet rather than its land.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Fitting a country onto your map",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Make neighbours sound different.</strong> A Norse-sounding kingdom next to a Latin empire tells the reader at once that these are two peoples, and probably old enemies.</li><li><strong>Match the towns.</strong> A country's towns should share its sound. Use the <a href=\"fantasy-town-name-generator.html\">Fantasy Town Name Generator</a> for English-style towns, and keep a few syllables from the country name for its capital.</li><li><strong>Give it another name.</strong> Countries rarely call themselves what their neighbours call them: Germany is <em>Deutschland</em> to Germans and <em>Allemagne</em> to the French. One country with two names is cheap and very convincing worldbuilding.</li><li><strong>Say the adjective.</strong> You'll write about its people more than the country, so check how the name sounds with <em>-ian</em>, <em>-ish</em> or <em>-er</em> on the end before you settle on it.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Are these names from Tolkien or Game of Thrones?",
        "answer": "No. They're built from sounds in the style of each language, and famous fictional and real country names that the parts happen to make are left out. Middle-earth is a model for the elvish style, but the names are new."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between the styles?",
        "answer": "Each uses the sounds of a different language: flowing elvish names, Norse names ending in -heim and -gard, Latin names ending in -ia and -ium, Old English kingdoms like Mercia, harsh names for dark lands, and island names ending in -holm and -ey."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use it as a kingdom name generator?",
        "answer": "Yes. Choose \"With a title\" to get kingdoms, empires, grand duchies and more, or use a name on its own and add your own title. The style decides how the kingdom sounds; the title decides how it is ruled."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I name the people of a fantasy country?",
        "answer": "Add an ending and say it aloud: -ian (Severican), -er (Valdheimer), -ish (Aelfwoldish) or -i (Nekothi). Pick whichever is easiest to say, and use it consistently."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D or my book?",
